WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
Film Courage: A producer gave you&nbsp;
some advice that was pretty lasting?

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:08.320
Shaun Paul Piccinino, Filmmaker: Yeah, I was a&nbsp;
personal trainer at the time and I was still I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:14.640
doing short films and all of that stuff. I think I&nbsp;
was making my first feature which was a back alley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:24.240
group of friends putting their credit cards into&nbsp;
the pot and just making a making a film. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:31.360
I was doing that at the time and he was somebody&nbsp;
who was very integral developing the Fast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:38.560
Furious franchise as well as the show 24. He&nbsp;
just happened to be one of my clients and and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.760
we became friends from the whole thing&nbsp;
and he was gave me some great advice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.120
One of the things he he told me was number one he&nbsp;
said don't be a meanie that was one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.160
rules so this business is so small that if you're&nbsp;
a jerk it's going to come back around so that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.800
one of the first things he told me he's like and&nbsp;
he was very colorful in the way he explained it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:12.400
but he's like that pa that you're working with on&nbsp;
set one week a year later they might be producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.040
the show that you're working on it's that that's&nbsp;
the way this business works so if you're a jerk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.320
to that extra they might be the star of some&nbsp;
show in a year or two so you just never you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.640
should never treat people poorly and especially&nbsp;
I mean that's just in life but especially in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.240
business because it is really so small you're&nbsp;
end up going to have to work with the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.000
people again most likely and the other thing he&nbsp;
told me was if I really wanted to pursue being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:50.160
creator a storyteller a filmmaker to never&nbsp;
be a no job is too small never be afraid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.680
of being the coffee runner or being the in charge&nbsp;
of extras or whatever it is and also to diversify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.920
so the business was changing so much at that&nbsp;
time it was when digital was just coming out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:11.920
it was kind of taking over everything and even&nbsp;
dvds and that whole blu-ray everything that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.160
the industry was just shifting and shifting and&nbsp;
shifting so he told me to diversify or die those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.440
were his exact words if you want to make it&nbsp;
and survive in this business diversify or die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.480
and I took that to heart and that's why if&nbsp;
you look up my credits you'll see I've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.960
so many different jobs is because&nbsp;
I basically adopted the attitude of

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.280
Just not turning down a job so if I was offered a&nbsp;
job and I had the ability to do it and I had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.520
time to do it that I would do it and and&nbsp;
again it goes back to that viewing it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.400
an education he's like oh I'm going to learn&nbsp;
about this and he also told me that same thing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:01.120
look if you learn what it is to be a grip and&nbsp;
if you learn what what it takes to be the the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.960
art department if you learn those things it's&nbsp;
going to make you a better director because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.600
you'll understand what it takes to do those jobs&nbsp;
and you'll understand what they're going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:18.320
to do those jobs in the same perspective it's very&nbsp;
important for a director to be understand acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.920
because you'll then be able to understand the&nbsp;
process or understand that there is a process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:30.160
with actors and be able to sympathize with them&nbsp;
or help enable them tell their truth in that role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.200
same thing with editing as a director I think&nbsp;
it's so important to understand editing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.720
you understand what you're shooting there are&nbsp;
directors who are not editors but I think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.240
need to have a certain understanding of that&nbsp;
in order to be effective it helps you with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.720
time management on set it helps you when you&nbsp;
have your scene you don't need to shoot four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.240
more angles that you'll never use and be on the&nbsp;
cutting room floor because you have the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.720
it's there you've got all the pieces you need so&nbsp;
those two things I already knew were important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.160
be a director but I didn't think about the other&nbsp;
stuff the the what is the gaffer doing what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:14.160
grips doing what are the makeup and hair and kind&nbsp;
of learning all those different departments it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.440
only going to help you become a better director&nbsp;
is what he told me and and so I took that to heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.440
and I took every job I could take I did everything&nbsp;
I could do and and just felt like it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:32.640
an education for me and and would just help&nbsp;
me be better at the job that I was trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:42.640
pursue do you ever feel guilty turning down work&nbsp;
you mean now yeah my sense is that you're eager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.280
and you love what you do that if if an opportunity&nbsp;
comes up you almost feel bad if you can't take it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.800
because maybe totally committed to something else&nbsp;
yes yes yeah for sure and what's interesting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:02.240
and topical right now is that I had to back out of&nbsp;
directing a film overseas because of the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:09.120
and actually I was over there when it started&nbsp;
getting really bad I was over there prepping and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:16.640
it's going to be a fantastic film I am now kind&nbsp;
of jealous that I didn't get to direct it but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.840
when this whole thing first came up I started&nbsp;
getting bad and and was definitely really worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:33.120
about getting home because it became one of those&nbsp;
things of at the time they're like what you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.200
just stay a couple extra weeks and wait it out&nbsp;
and then when it's safe to travel again it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.200
one of those and but what what ended up happening&nbsp;
was the international flight started cancelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.440
like just stopping all international flights and&nbsp;
I went oof I think I'm going to have to go home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:54.560
because I can't get stuck here I've got a family&nbsp;
and all that so I did I ended up getting home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.880
and the last weekend where those&nbsp;
international flights were flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:08.480
I got home and did the quarantine did all&nbsp;
that stuff got tested and I was fine but the

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.760
it the whole thing got pushed because of it and&nbsp;
but then it came down to this thing when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.880
wanted to shoot it still was just not a safe time&nbsp;
to be to go and travel and and the worry of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:30.480
able to get home was so just too big of a worry&nbsp;
it was just too much to be able to commit and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.160
there and do it and I felt horrible turning that&nbsp;
down because I had committed to doing this project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:44.400
with with these wonderful filmmakers and some of&nbsp;
which I had really dreamed about working with for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:52.240
a long time because they were a big part of asian&nbsp;
cinema like with with Jet Ley and Jackie Chan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:07:00.000
all that and so I had to turn it down I felt so&nbsp;
so bad but obviously it's not under my control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.360
it was out of my control the whole situation&nbsp;
so but yeah I think I do feel that way because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:17.120
I just enjoy I enjoy creating and helping tell&nbsp;
stories in whatever the capacity whether it's even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.840
if it's stunt coordinating that's still your part&nbsp;
of telling that story you're part of telling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.560
the story of the action of how it's happening and&nbsp;
unfolding and I just love I love that process so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.760
and I think there's also that element of&nbsp;
feeling like you might be able to help somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:45.360
and then not being able to having to say no I&nbsp;
can't help you because I get those those calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:50.800
fairly often it's it's somebody usually when&nbsp;
they're calling me because they need your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:58.320
piece of expertise or something that you might&nbsp;
be able to be that puzzle piece for them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.600
then when you're not available obviously&nbsp;
that's that sucks but I always try and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.160
recommend somebody I'm like wait call this person&nbsp;
or I'm definitely that that way as well I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:25.840
love to to if I can't do it get somebody&nbsp;
else the work what I mean and so I do that.

